{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## PROBE-4B9E-R2: ROOT CAUSE FOUND. `serverInfo` 200 is NOT readiness for Jira Software custom fields.\n\nRun 30944211742, `main` @ b868ea914. Still `PROBE SETUP FAILED: Epic Link=None Epic Name=None` — but\nthis time the evidence survived, which is exactly what Gerrit 1373 was for. **One run, from\nundiagnosable to diagnosed.**\n\n### The diagnostic the fix added\n\n```\nfield 'Epic Link' absent from an inventory of 27 field(s); names present:\n  ['Archived','Archiver','Attachment','Comment','Created','Creator','Images','Key','Last Viewed',\n   'Log Work','Original Estimate','Progress','Project','Remaining Estimate','Resolved','Status',\n   'Sub-Tasks','Time Spent','Time Tracking','Updated','Votes','Watchers','Work Ratio',\n   'Σ Original Estimate','Σ Progress','Σ Remaining Estimate','Σ Time Spent']\nABORTED — wrote 2 recorded round trip(s) to .../evidence.json before exiting\n```\n\nThe inventory is **27 fields and every one is a SYSTEM field.** Not one `customfield_*`. So the field\nlist was readable and authorised — the request worked fine — and Jira Software's GreenHopper fields\n(`Epic Link`, `Epic Name`) simply **had not been registered yet**. My earlier hypothesis that a missing\n`Accept` header was producing a non-list body was wrong; the body was a perfectly good list of the\nwrong contents.\n\n### The timing proves it\n\n```\nWait for Jira DC to become ready : 19:39:01 -> 19:47:33   (8m32s, polling /rest/api/2/serverInfo)\nRun the Epic-Link clear probe    : 19:47:33 -> 19:47:34   (1 SECOND later)\n```\n\n`serverInfo` answered 200 after 8m32s; **one second later** the custom fields were still absent.\n\n### Why the harness never hit this, and why that is its own latent bug\n\nBoth workflows use the IDENTICAL readiness gate — `.github/workflows/jira-dc-epic-link-probe.yml:50-60`\nand `.github/workflows/external-integration.yml:526-536` both poll `serverInfo` until 200. The harness\nis not more careful; it is just SLOWER afterwards. It installs dependencies and then runs a 41-minute\nsuite, so by the time `conftest._assert_project_capabilities` checks for `Epic Link` / `Epic Name`,\nminutes have passed and the plugin is up. The probe queries in the first second and sees the truth.\n\n**So the harness's declared-environment contract (bug 3fe5) is asserted against a readiness signal too\nweak to guarantee it.** If a future run reached that fixture faster, or the plugin initialised slower,\n`_assert_project_capabilities` would fail at provisioning and report it as the 3fe5 image degrade —\n\"the image no longer yields the declared environment\" — when the real cause is timing. That is a\nmisattributing flake waiting to happen, and it is the same family as 3fe5 itself: a contract checked at\nthe wrong moment. Filed separately.\n\n### The fix for this probe\n\nWait for the CAPABILITY the probe actually needs, not a proxy for it: poll `/rest/api/2/field` until\n`Epic Link` appears, under its own bounded budget, and fail with the inventory dump if it never does.\n`serverInfo` is the wrong predicate for anything that depends on a plugin's fields.\n\n**4b9e's blocking unknown is STILL UNANSWERED** — whether `{epic_link_field_id: None}` actually clears\non DC 8.17.1. The Rank 1 cell stays unwritten. But the obstacle is now a known, small, fixable\nreadiness bug rather than a mystery, and each attempt no longer costs a blind boot cycle."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785873004288932001,"uuid":"30a5dcfb-0f81-4d26-bcea-45860dcb21a2"}